      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "In order for the polynomial $P(x)$ to have all the elements listed above (each having multiplicity 1), each of those numbers must be a root of $P(x)$. For example, the critical root to consider is $s+ \\sqrt{p}$, where $p$ and $s$ are integers not equal. This means that the adjacent polynomial $Q(x)$ must also have a root $p+ \\sqrt{p}$ as well, since those polynomials have rational coefficients. Next, we note that the polynomials $Q(x)$ also have the roots $s+ \\sqrt{p},  s+2 \\sqrt{p}, . . ,  s+ 250 \\sqrt{p}$. When taking the product of all of these polynomials, we get all the necessary roots. The degree of the polynomial $Q(x)$ is $2(p+1)$. Due to the symmetry of this pattern, we can get the degree of $P(x)$ as:   $$2(1+2+3+ . . .+1000) + 1000 + 1001 = 2 (1000 * 1001 / 2) + 1000 + 1001 = 2012016$$\n\nThe answer is 2012016.",
